 "summary": "Gates S1 to S4 all passed (port difference: 0 status changes (0 allowed exceptions), 0 failure changes; control difference: 1 status changes; cases over 0.25 dB: 34 port, 40 control; constraint verdict true to false: 0; nonlinear verdicts differing: 0 port, 0 control).",
 "predefined_rules": {
  "status": "The rule as amended by Amendment 1; gates S1 to S3 of the first version (fixed 2026-10-14 00:55 +0300) are superseded, S4 stands.",
  "amendment_1": {
   "number": 1,
   "fixed_at": "2026-10-14 01:48 +0300",
   "predates_any_judged_case": true,
   "reason": "A parity study on 360 synthetic seeded fits showed the incumbent is not stable against itself: with every solver start value moved by one unit in the last place, SciPy's fitted gains move by up to 5.2 dB in the worst fit, constraint_solver_success flips in 20 of 360 fits, and SLSQP fails outright in 14 calls. Gates S1 to S3 as first written (rule fixed 2026-10-14 00:55 +0300) would measure SciPy's noise, not the port, so they are replaced; S4 stands."
  },
  "question": "Do the Auto-EQ fits computed with the NumPy solvers (bounded least squares, gain projection, constrained gain refinement) match the fits computed with SciPy's least_squares and SLSQP closely enough that removing SciPy from the runtime changes no recommendation and nothing audible?",
  "control arm": "A third arm runs the incumbent source with SciPy, with the start vector of every least_squares and minimize call moved by one unit in the last place (numpy.nextafter toward +infinity, clipped into the bounds). \"Control difference\" means control arm versus incumbent arm; \"port difference\" means candidate arm versus incumbent arm.",
  "S1 same_status": "Failure status is identical on every case in the port difference. A recommendation-status change in the port difference is an allowed exception when the case moves from \"reduced\" to \"apply\", its only recommendation reason in the incumbent is the constraint-solver one, the incumbent's constraint solve failed (iteration cap or solver exit failure) or its result was rejected by the feasibility check, and the candidate's result is feasible. Every exception is listed. The number of other recommendation-status changes in the port difference is at most the number of recommendation-status changes in the control difference.",
  "S2 same_response": "On cases outside the S1 exceptions that produce an EQ in both arms compared: at least 90 % of cases in the port difference are within 0.05 dB at every frequency; and for each threshold T in {0.05, 0.25, 1.0} dB, the number of cases exceeding T in the port difference is at most twice the number exceeding T in the control difference plus 5.",
  "S3 same_solver_verdicts": "constraint_solver_success never changes from true to false in the port difference; a change from false to true requires a feasible candidate result. The number of cases where nonlinear_solver_success differs in the port difference is at most twice the number in the control difference plus 2.",
  "S4 exceptions_are_small": "For each S1 exception the new arm's final response is within 1.0 dB of the SciPy arm's response before SciPy's result was rejected... if that intermediate is not observable, within 1.0 dB of the SciPy arm's final (\"reduced\") response scaled back to full strength; if neither can be computed the case is reported and counts as a failure of this gate.",
  "decision": "SciPy leaves the runtime only if S1 to S4 all pass. Otherwise SciPy stays for this release and the evidence is filed under models/evaluation-details/auto-eq-solver-port-2026-10/. A failed gate is not reinterpreted after the run; a solver bug found on the development passages may be fixed and the whole comparison rerun, and each rerun is recorded."
 },
